readme='''# City into Sea — native Studio review

[Watch the eight-second film](City%20into%20Sea.mp4) · [Live Studio timeline](Complete%20live%20Studio%20playback.mp4) · [Finished movie beside its editable timeline](Complete%20finished%20playback%20in%20Studio.mp4) · [Edit the camera](Edit%20the%20camera%20in%20Studio.mp4).

The street becomes a cobalt stage. The walking person separates from the city, gains a vermilion echo, fills with moving ocean, then opens into that same ocean. Ten ordinary layers and 543 keys remain editable, including a shared camera rig, exact source-bound video mattes and a 64-point iris. Every evaluated effect is in the native Studio edit and canonical CPU export. The native scene-data import was explicitly art directed by the assistant; **Director did not autonomously create this successful edit**. Its failed/rejected model attempts remain recorded.

The movie is 1280×720, 24fps, exactly 192 frames / eight seconds, H.264/AAC. Originals, static graphics, score, matching native backup, native Apply/history/edit recordings, cut/pose contact sheet and nine paired live/export samples are included. The original exported backup matches its render receipt exactly. A fresh demo restores the same hash-verified inputs after the old temporary runtime was cleared; that does not restore its job/wallet history. The movie was not rendered again.

Two preview defects were repaired: Quick cold reopen omitted video mattes, and shared mattes could read stale clone-decoder pixels. Nine new paused pairs show the missing figures restored; 36 effective source timestamps match exactly. Whole pixel/audio equality and real-time smoothness are not claimed. Recordings are muted QA captures; listen to the separate finished film. Owner picture/listening approval remains open. Nothing was published, scheduled, deployed or purchased.
'''
(out/'README.md').write_text(readme)
